Theorem TF-10a: Reset transfer produces SCALAR sentinel (reuse-token handle)

  Preconditions:
    - Instruction is `Reset { var }` per Annex E §AIMS TF-10a +
      Appendix A row 12
    - `Reset` emits an opaque reuse-token handle consumed by paired
      `Reuse` (RL-11 same-block) or `Reuse` (RL-12 cross-block); the
      token is NOT reference-counted at the AIMS layer
    - Token-liveness demand (TF-11 emits `(token, Once)` at Reuse)
      prevents dead-code elimination — proven at §04.3 TF-11; §04.2
      TF-10a proves only the forward-transfer SCALAR row
    - Structural pairing of Reset/Reuse validated at §08 RL-11/RL-12

  Soundness property:
    Forall Reset { var }.
      transfer_tf10a(Reset) = SCALAR
    AND L-6 layer (b) monotonicity holds for the constant function
    f(_) = SCALAR.

  Proof obligation:
    Two-part discharge:
      (a) Output verification — TF-10a emits `dst := SCALAR` regardless
          of `var`'s AimsState. Appendix A row 12 shows SCALAR across
          every per-dimension column. The reuse token is an opaque
          handle (not RC-tracked, no lattice-element representation).
      (b) L-6 layer (b) monotonicity — TF-10a is a constant function:
          for ANY pair of input states (a, b) with a ≤ b,
          f(a) = f(b) = SCALAR; by L-4 reflexivity SCALAR ≤ SCALAR,
          hence f(a) ≤ f(b).

    Part (a) — SCALAR-output verification:
      The Reset result is a reuse-token handle. Per Appendix A row 12 +
      Annex E §AIMS TF-10a + §1.8 L-9, the token is the SCALAR
      sentinel — no RC, no lattice-element representation.
      Verified: transfer_tf10a() emits Tagged::Scalar regardless of
      input AimsState. The token is consumed by a paired Reuse
      instruction (RL-11 same-block or RL-12 cross-block); TF-11
      backward demand emits `(token, Once)` at the Reuse site to
      prevent dead-code elimination — that liveness obligation is
      proven at §04.3 TF-11 and is OUT-OF-SCOPE for this forward-
      transfer proof.
      Note: per Annex E §AIMS TF-10a, the token has L-9 SCALAR
      property at AIMS level despite being a Reset-Reuse coupling
      mechanism; the structural pairing is enforced via the §08
      RL-11/RL-12 rules + emission-time validation, not the forward
      transfer.

    Part (b) — constant-function monotonicity:
      Constant f(_) = SCALAR is trivially monotone: forall a, b.
      f(a) = f(b) = SCALAR, and SCALAR ≤ SCALAR holds definitionally
      per L-4 reflexivity. Same argument as TF-10 part (b).

    Engines dispatched:
      monotonicity (PRIMARY — L-6 layer (b) per-rule discharge per
        section_04::verify_tf10a_reset_scalar — constant-function
        check)
      case_analysis (PRIMARY — Appendix A row 12 forward-transfer
        enumeration per section_04::verify_tf10a_reset_scalar)
      lattice (SECONDARY — accepts gracefully; L-9 SCALAR-not-a-
        lattice-element discharged at §02)
      refinement (SECONDARY — accepts gracefully; TF-10a emits SCALAR
        directly, not via refine())

  Expected:
    status: valid
    reason: section_04 monotonicity + case_analysis engines discharge TF-10a via SCALAR-output verification + constant-function monotonicity check
